    {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Reading this dashboard",
      "id": 24,
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 73
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "\nEvery panel here reads the **raw `flows` table**, because it is the only one that\nkeeps `SrcAddr`, `DstAddr`, `SrcPort` and `DstPort` \u2014 Akvorado drops all four\nfrom `flows_1m0s`, `flows_5m0s` and `flows_1h0s`. That bounds this whole\ndashboard to the raw TTL, **15 days by default**. Widen\n`clickhouse.resolutions[0].ttl` in Akvorado if you need to answer questions\nolder than that.\n\n`$addr` is a plain text box rather than a dropdown, so it takes any address \u2014\nincluding one from an abuse report that has never appeared here before. The\ncustomer tables on **Traffic & 95th Percentile** link straight into this\ndashboard with the address and time range carried over.\n\n**Counts are sampled.** At 1:1000 a destination only appears if one of its\npackets was sampled, so \"distinct destinations\" and the conversation table are\nsystematically short. Bytes and packets are corrected by `SamplingRate` and are\nunbiased; distinct counts are not correctable and should be read as shape.\n\nNothing here filters by `SrcNetRole`, on purpose: an address that ought to be a\ncustomer but is not tagged in Akvorado's `networks:` config would otherwise\nsilently return nothing.\n"
      }
    }
